tional effects of nicotinic agonists in rats. <source>Neuropharmacology.</source> <volume>44</volume>:<fpage>1054</fpage>&#x02013;1067.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12763099</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib16"><citation citation-type="journal">Hamill, O.P., A. Marty, E. Neher, B. Sakmann, and F.J. Sigworth. <year>1981</year>. Improved patch-clamp techniques for high-resolution current recording from cells and cell-free membrane patches. <source>Pflugers Arch.</source> <volume>391</volume>:<fpage>85</fpage>&#x02013;100.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">6270629</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib17"><citation citation-type="journal">Hatton, C.J., C. Shelley, M. Brydson, D. Beeson, and D. Colquhoun. <year>2003</year>. Properties of the human muscle nicotinic receptor, and of the slow-channel myasthenic syndrome mutant &#x0025b;L221F, inferred from maximum likelihood fits. <source>J. Physiol.</source> <volume>547</volume>:<fpage>729</fpage>&#x02013;760.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12562900</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib18"><citation citation-type="journal">Holladay, M.W., M.J. Dart, and J.K. Lynch. <year>1997</year>. Neuronal nicotinic acetylcholine receptors as targets for drug discovery. <source>J. Med. Chem.</source> <volume>40</volume>:<fpage>4169</fpage>&#x02013;4194.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9435889</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib19"><citation citation-type="journal">Jackson, M.B. <year>1984</year>. Spontaneous openings of the acetylcholine receptor channel. <source>Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. USA.</source> <volume>81</volume>:<fpage>3901</fpage>&#x02013;3904.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">6328531</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib20"><citation citation-type="journal">Jackson, M.B. <year>1994</year>. Single channel currents in the nicotinic acetylcholine receptor: a direct demonstration of allosteric transitions. <source>Trends Biochem. Sci.</source> <volume>19</volume>:<fpage>396</fpage>&#x02013;399.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7529435</pub-id></citation></ref><