</fpage>&#x02013;1501.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">14990476</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib33"><citation citation-type="journal">Qin, F., and L. Li. <year>2004</year>. Model-based fitting of single-channel dwell-time distributions. <source>Biophys. J.</source> <volume>87</volume>:<fpage>1657</fpage>&#x02013;1671.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15345545</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib34"><citation citation-type="journal">Qin, F., A. Auerbach, and F. Sachs. <year>1996</year>. Estimating single-channel kinetic parameters from idealized patch-clamp data containing missed events. <source>Biophys. J.</source> <volume>70</volume>:<fpage>264</fpage>&#x02013;280.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8770203</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib35"><citation citation-type="journal">Roux, B., and R. Sauv&#x000e9;. <year>1985</year>. A general solution to the time interval omission problem applied to single channel analysis. <source>Biophys. J.</source> <volume>48</volume>:<fpage>149</fpage>&#x02013;158.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">2410047</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib36"><citation citation-type="journal">Romanelli, M.N., and F. Gualtieri. <year>2003</year>. Cholinergic nicotinic receptors: competitive ligands, allosteric modulators, and their potential applications. <source>Med. Res. Rev.</source> <volume>23</volume>:<fpage>393</fpage>&#x02013;426.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12710018</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib37"><citation citation-type="journal">Rothberg, B.S., and K.L. Magleby. <year>1998</year>. Kinetic structure of large-conductance Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>-activated K<sup>&#x0002b;</sup> channels suggests that the gating includes transitions through intermediate or secondary states. A mechanism for flickers. <source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source> <volume>111</volume>:<fpage>751</fpage>&#x02013;780.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9607935</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib38"><citation citation-type="journal">Salamone, F.N., M. Zhou, and A. Auerbach. <year>1999</year>. A re-examination of adult mouse nicotinic acetylcholine receptor channel activation kinetics. <source>J. Physiol.</source> <volume>516</volume>:<fpage>315</fpage>&#x02013;330.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10087333</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib39"><citation citation-type="journal">Schwarz, G. <year>1978</year>. Estimating the dimension of a model. <source>Ann. Stat.</source> <volume>6</volume>:<fpage>461</fpage>&#x02013;464.</citation></ref><ref id="bib40"><citation citation-type="journal">Sine, S.M. <year>1993</year>. Molecular dissection of subunit interfaces in the acetylcholine receptor: identification of residues that determine curare selectivity. <source>Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. USA.</source> <volume>90</volume>:<fpage>9436</fpage>&#x02013;9440.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8415719</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib41"><citation citation-type="journal">Sine, S.M., T. Claudio, and F.J. Sigworth. <year>1990</year>. Activation of Torpedo acetylcholine receptors expressed in mouse fibroblasts: single channel current kinetics reveal distinct agonist binding affinities. <source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source> <volume>96</volume>:<fpage>395</fpage>&#x02013;437.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">1698917</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib42"><citation citation-type="journal">Teague, S.J. <year>2003</year>. Implications of protein flexibility for drug discovery. <source>Nat. Rev. Drug Discov.</source> <volume>2</volume>:<fpage>527</fpage>&#x02013;541.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12838268</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib43"><citation citation-type="journal">Wang, H.-L., A. Auerbach, N. Bren, K. Ohno, A.G. Engel, and S.M. Sine. <year>1997</year>. Mutation in the M1 domain of the acetylcholine receptor &#x003b1; subunit decreases the rate of agonist dissociation. <source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source> <volume>109</volume>:<fpage>757</fpage>&#x02013;766.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9222901</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib44"><citation citation-type="journal">Woo, H.-J., and B. Roux. <year>2005</year>. Calculation of absolute protein-ligand binding free energy from computer simulations. <source>Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. USA.</source> <volume>102</volume>:<fpage>6825</fpage>&#x02013;6830.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15867154</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib45"><citation citation-type="journal">Zhang, Y., J. Chen, and A. Auerbach. <year>1995</year>. Activation of recombinant mouse acetylcholine receptors by acetylcholine, carbamylcholine and tetramethylammonium. <source>J. Physiol.</source> <volume>486</volume>:<fpage>189</fpage>&#x02013;206.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7562635</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list><app-group><app id="app1"><title>APPENDIX</title><p>We tested the assumption that the two AChR transmitter binding sites are functionally equivalent and independent (in terms of their K<sub>D</sub> values) by fitting the &#x003b1;S269I&#x02013;choline one-dimensional single-channel P<sub>open</sub> curve with the four possible models. We tested this assumption on the &#x003b1;S269I mutant, rather than on the wild type, because the interaction between this mutant and choline can be characterized with a simple 1-D concentration&#x02013;response relationship (<xref rid="fig12" ref-type="fig">Fig. 12</xref>), which greatly facilitates the analysis.</p><p>For a receptor with two binding sites, and in the presence of a single ligand, the most general kinetic scheme (omitting unliganded openings, monoliganded openings, and desensitized and blocked states) is given in <xref rid="fig15" ref-type="fig">Fig. 15</xref>. The properties of equivalence and independence of the transmitter binding sites can be combined to give rise to four models, each of which is characterized by a different P<sub>open</sub> expression.</p><fig position="float" id="fig15"><label>Figure 15.</label><caption><p>Kinetic scheme used to test the assumption of equivalence and independence of the transmitter binding sites in term of their K<sub>D</sub> values. A denotes the agonist (here, choline), CA and AC denote the two configurations of the closed monoliganded receptor, and ACA and AOA denote the closed and open forms of the diliganded receptor. Different constraints can be applied to the four dissociation equilibrium constants (K<sub>D1</sub>&#x02013;K<sub>D4</sub>) to account for different binding site properties (see Appendix). The inset shows the definitions of the equilibrium constants in the model.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="jgp1270719f15"></graphic></fig><p>If the two binding sites are equivalent and independent, then (<xref rid="fig15" ref-type="fig">Fig. 15</xref>) K<sub>D1</sub> &#x0003d; K<sub>D2</sub> &#x0003d; K<sub>D3</sub> &#x0003d; K<sub>D4</sub> &#x0003d; K<sub>D</sub>, and the P<sub>open</sub> (here, P<sub>AOA</sub>) is given by:<disp-formula id="fd9"><label>(9)</label><tex-math id="M8">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}{\mathrm{P}}_{{\mathrm{AOA}}}=\frac{1}{1+\displaystyle\frac{1}{{\theta}_{2}}+\displaystyle\frac{2{\mathrm{K}}_{{\mathrm{D}}}}{{\theta}_{2}{\mathrm{A}}}+\displaystyle\frac{{\mathrm{K}}_{{\mathrm{D}}}^{2}}{{\theta}_{2}{\mathrm{A}}^{2}}},\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>where A denotes the ligand concentration, and &#x003b8;<sub>2</sub> is the diliganded gating equilibrium constant. <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="fd9">Eq. 9</xref> is, of course, simply another way of writing <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="fd7">Eq. 7</xref>, but this form is useful for comparison with the expressions corresponding to the models below.</p><p>If the two binding sites are different and independent, then K<sub>D1</sub> &#x0003d; K<sub>D4</sub> &#x0003d; K<sub>DA</sub> and K<sub>D2</sub> &#x0003d; K<sub>D3</sub> &#x0003d; K<sub>DB</sub>, and the P<sub>open</sub> is given by:<disp-formula id="fd10"><label>(10)</label><tex-math id="M9">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}{\mathrm{P}}_{{\mathrm{AOA}}}=\frac{1}{1+\displaystyle\frac{1}{{\theta}_{2}}+\displaystyle\frac{({\mathrm{K}}_{{\mathrm{DA}}}+{\mathrm{K}}_{{\mathrm{DB}}}{\mathrm{)}}}{{\theta}_{2}{\mathrm{A}}}+\displaystyle\frac{{\mathrm{K}}_{{\mathrm{DA}}}{\mathrm{K}}_{{\mathrm{DB}}}}{{\theta}_{2}{\mathrm{A}}^{2}}},\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>where K<sub>DA</sub> and K<sub>DB</sub> are the ligand dissociation equilibrium constants from either binding site regardless of whether both or only one site is occupied.</p><p>If the two binding sites are equivalent and interacting, then K<sub>D1</sub> &#x0003d; K<sub>D2</sub> &#x0003d; K<sub>MONO</sub> and K<sub>D3</sub> &#x0003d; K<sub>D4</sub> &#x0003d; K<sub>DOUBLY</sub>, and the P<sub>open</sub> is given by:<disp-formula id="fd11"><label>(11)</label><tex-math id="M10">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}{\mathrm{P}}_{{\mathrm{AOA}}}=\frac{1}{1+\displaystyle\frac{1}{{\theta}_{2}}+\displaystyle\frac{2{\mathrm{K}}_{{\mathrm{DOUBLY}}}}{{\theta}_{2}{\mathrm{A}}}+\displaystyle\frac{{\mathrm{K}}_{{\mathrm{MONO}}}{\mathrm{K}}_{{\mathrm{DOUBLY}}}}{{\theta}_{2}{\mathrm{A}}^{2}}},\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>where K<sub>MONO</sub> is the ligand dissociation equilibrium constant from either monoliganded receptor configuration (both configurations are equivalent), and K<sub>DOUBLY</sub> is the ligand dissociation equilibrium constant from either site of the diliganded receptor.</p><p>Finally, if the two binding sites are different and interacting, the P<sub>open</sub> is given by:<disp-formula id="fd12"><label>(12)</label><tex-math id="M11">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}{\mathrm{P}}_{{\mathrm{AOA}}}=\frac{1}{1+\displaystyle\frac{1}{{\theta}_{2}}+\displaystyle\frac{{\mathrm{K}}_{{\mathrm{D3}}}}{{\theta}_{2}{\mathrm{A}}} \left \left(1+\displaystyle\frac{{\mathrm{K}}_{{\mathrm{D}}1}}{{\mathrm{K}}_{{\mathrm{D}}2}}\right) \right +\displaystyle\frac{{\mathrm{K}}_{{\mathrm{D}}1}{\mathrm{K}}_{{\mathrm{D3}}}}{{\theta}_{2}{\mathrm{A}}^{2}}},\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>where K<sub>D4</sub> is expressed in terms of K<sub>D1</sub>, K<sub>D2</sub>, and K<sub>D3</sub> (K<sub>D4</sub> &#x0003d; K<sub>D1</sub>K<sub>D3</sub>/K<sub>D2</sub>, from detailed balance).</p><p>The parameter estimates obtained using <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="fd12">Eq. 12</xref> (i.e., assuming that the binding sites are different and interacting) were very sensitive to the initial guesses, and the three K<sub>D</sub> estimates (here, K<sub>D1</sub>, K<sub>D2</sub>, and K<sub>D3</sub>) had very large coefficients of variation. The results of fitting the data with the other three models, using <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="fd9">Eqs. 9</xref>&#x02013;<xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="fd11">11</xref>, are summarized in <xref rid="fig12" ref-type="fig">Fig. 12</xref> and <xref ref-type="table" rid="tbl2">Table II</xref>.</p></app></app-group></back></article>